190
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:40,880
But what that ends up meaning is that I'm very sensitive to the delay through this cloud of logic, because any computation happening in there needs to finish before the next clock cycle hits.

191
00:41:40,880 --> 00:42:02,800
A major point of optimization on any chip is to make this delay as short as possible. Interesting. The constraint here seems to be that if you add too much logic,

192
00:42:02,800 --> 00:42:15,840
you might risk missing the clock cycle. But if you don't add enough, you're leaving potential compute on the table. Is there ever a situation where you take

193
00:42:15,840 --> 00:42:28,720
a probabilistic chance that a computation finishes, or is it strictly that it either finishes by the clock cycle or it doesn't? In standard chip design, you margin it such

194
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:40,720
that there is a probability, but it's many standard deviations out. For all intents and purposes, it is a reliable part and will always meet the clock.

195
00:42:40,720 --> 00:42:47,600
