293
01:04:43,920 --> 01:05:09,200
In a CPU, you have the CPU die itself, and then DDR memory off on the side. You have a cache system inside that remembers recent accesses to DDR and stores them.

294
01:05:09,200 --> 01:05:17,600
When I'm running through my CPU instructions, every time I have an instruction that accesses memory, it first checks if the data was stored in the cache.

295
01:05:17,600 --> 01:05:27,200
If not, it fetches it from DDR. This is a huge optimization. The cache is two orders of magnitude faster than the DDR.

296
01:05:27,200 --> 01:05:39,360
If you never used the cache, basically all programs would run a hundred times slower. The presence of a cache is absolutely necessary for a CPU to run at a reasonable speed.

297
01:05:39,360 --> 01:05:48,880
But whether or not you get a cache hit depends on the ambient environment of the CPU: what other programs are running, what has run recently, and what the random number

298
01:05:48,880 --> 01:06:01,600
generator inside the cache system is doing. That is a big source of non-determinism in the runtime of a CPU. That is the memory system for a CPU.

299
01:06:01,600 --> 01:06:11,280
The big thing you can do differently is, instead of having the hardware say, "I’m going to read memory" and then the hardware decides whether or not it comes

